probe - hand the kernel the arguments a broken program would

Every case here is a call that must come back as an error rather than as a
dead machine or a granted power: a null pointer, a pointer nothing is mapped
at, an address in the kernel's own half, a length that wraps, a buffer that
runs off the end of its page, a read-only page offered as somewhere to
write, a handle nobody was given, a message at an address no message could
sit at, one of the system's own service names, an event given only to read,
and a program image whose bytes reach past its own file.

A kernel that reads a user pointer without checking the pages behind it are
there faults in its own context, and a fault in kernel context stops the
machine. Any program could then stop it with a stray pointer, which is the
difference between a bug in a program and a bug in the system. The rest are
the same question about authority rather than about memory: what a program
may take, and what it may only be given.

It then sends handles over a channel a few hundred times, to itself, and
reads how much the kernel is holding before and after. A reference kept on
every call is an object that is never freed, which nothing else would show:
no program can ask how many references an object has, but it can ask how
much memory has not come back.

It says how many were refused as they should be. Anything other than all of
them is a hole, and a machine that stops partway through has the worst kind.

`probe echo` is the other end of that channel and is not meant to be run by
hand: the handle cases need something to call, and a server that answers is
the smallest thing that will do.
